window.docContents[4].resolve({"/Units-and-the-Dimension-1-Problem/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--Units-and-the-Dimension-1-Problem":{"contents":"This chapter states the layer that originally motivated PropertyKindCalculus. Its\nmotivating capstone — the dimension-1 disambiguation — is now proved (see the\nDimension as a Forgetful Functor chapter for the forgetful map it rests on);\nthe unit-arithmetic nodes around it remain planned, carrying\ninformal statements and proof sketches that appear as in-progress goals in the\ndependency graph until a (lean := …) declaration or checked code block is\nattached.\n\nPhysLib's Dimension (a ℚ-exponent free commutative group over the SI base\nquantities) makes every dimension-one quantity equal. Volumetric water content\n(), gravimetric water content (), relative\npermittivity, reflectivity, and emissivity are all dimension one — so a\ndimension-only type system cannot tell them apart. In soil-moisture retrieval\nthat is precisely the class of confusion that silently corrupts a result. The\nkind layer is what restores the distinction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PropertyKindCalculus Blueprint","header":"5. Units and the Dimension-1 Problem","id":"/Units-and-the-Dimension-1-Problem/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--Units-and-the-Dimension-1-Problem"}});